<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Epi-Olmec culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300 BCE–250 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Epi-Olmec culture followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmec civilisation</a> in the Gulf Coast lowlands of southern Veracruz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, from about 300 BCE to 250 CE, with centres such as Tres Zapotes and Cerro de las Mesas. It is known for an early writing system, the Isthmian or Epi-Olmec script, and for some of the earliest Long Count calendar dates, such as that on Stela C at Tres Zapotes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epi-olmec-culture.jpg" alt="Epi-Olmec culture" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Madman2001 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epi-Olmec_cultural_area.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
